.TH kill "" "Command"
.SH NAME
kill \- signal a process
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBkill [\-\fIsignal\fB] \fIpid ...\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
kill -- signal a process
Usage:	kill [ -signal ] pid ...
Send 'signal' (default: terminate signal) to each specified 'pid'.
.HE
.B kill
sends a signal to each process named by a
.IR pid .
By default it sends the software termination signal,
.BR SIGTERM .
.PP
If the first argument begins with
.BR \- ,
it names the signal to send instead of the default.
.I signal
may be given either as a decimal number, or as a signal name (with or
without the leading
.B SIG
prefix; case is not significant).
.B kill
recognizes the following signal names:
.DS
.ta 10n 20n 30n
\fBHUP\fR	\fBINT\fR	\fBQUIT\fR	\fBALRM\fR
\fBTERM\fR	\fBREST\fR	\fBSYS\fR	\fBPIPE\fR
\fBKILL\fR	\fBTRAP\fR	\fBSEGV\fR	\fBILL\fR
\fBIOT\fR	\fBEMT\fR	\fBFPE\fR	\fBBUS\fR
.DE
.PP
Each remaining argument is a
.I pid
that must begin with a decimal digit or a
.B \-
(the latter for a process-group id); anything else is rejected as
not being a process id.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
For each
.I pid
that does not begin with a digit or
.BR \- ,
.B kill
reports that it is not a process id and goes on to the next one.
If
.I signal
is not one of the names or numbers above,
.B kill
reports that there is no such signal and exits.
For a
.I pid
that exists but cannot be signaled (for example, because it is not
owned by the caller),
.B kill
prints the system error message and continues with the next
.IR pid .
.SH NOTES
.B kill
exits with a nonzero status if any signal could not be sent.
